            <!-- ==========================================
                 PART 1 - AUTHENTICATION
            ========================================== -->

            <section class="chapter" id="authentication-cheat-sheet">

                <h2>Authentication Cheat Sheet</h2>

                <p>
                    Authentication is the process of verifying
                    the identity of a user, device, application,
                    or other entity attempting to access a system.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Authentication helps protect user accounts,
                    personal information, and restricted resources
                    from unauthorized access.
                </p>

                <h3>Important Concepts</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Authentication:</strong>
                        Verifying an entity's claimed identity.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <strong>Digital Identity:</strong>
                        The representation of an entity within
                        an online service.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <strong>Identity Proofing:</strong>
                        Establishing that a claimed identity
                        corresponds to the intended subject.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <strong>Session Management:</strong>
                        Maintaining the authenticated state
                        of a user across multiple requests.
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Common Authentication Methods</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>Passwords and passphrases</li>
                    <li>One-time authentication codes</li>
                    <li>Hardware security keys</li>
                    <li>Passkeys</li>
                    <li>Client certificates</li>
                    <li>Multi-factor authentication</li>
                </ul>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="authentication-general-guidelines">

                <h2>Authentication General Guidelines</h2>

                <p>
                    Secure authentication requires more than
                    checking whether a password is correct.
                    Developers must protect credentials,
                    manage sessions, prevent automated attacks,
                    and handle sensitive account operations.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Authentication controls should be designed
                    to reduce the possibility of account takeover,
                    credential theft, and identity impersonation.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="authentication-user-ids">

                <h2>User IDs</h2>

                <p>
                    A User ID uniquely identifies an account
                    within an application.
                </p>

                <p>
                    OWASP recommends considering randomly
                    generated identifiers to reduce risks
                    associated with predictable sequential IDs.
                </p>

                <h3>Example</h3>

                <div class="code-example">
                    <pre><code>const crypto = require("node:crypto");

const userId = crypto.randomUUID();

console.log(userId);</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p>
                    Random identifiers make account identifiers
                    harder to predict, but they do not replace
                    authorization checks.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="authentication-usernames">

                <h2>Usernames</h2>

                <p>
                    A username is an identifier used to
                    recognize a user during authentication.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A username may be different from the
                    application's internal User ID.
                </p>

                <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>Allow meaningful usernames where appropriate.</li>
                    <li>Support verified email addresses as login identifiers.</li>
                    <li>Enforce username uniqueness when required.</li>
                    <li>Apply consistent validation rules.</li>
                    <li>Avoid exposing sensitive account information.</li>
                </ul>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="sensitive-authentication-accounts">

                <h2>Authentication Solution and Sensitive Accounts</h2>

                <p>
                    Highly privileged accounts used for
                    infrastructure, databases, middleware,
                    and administrative services require
                    stronger separation from ordinary
                    user authentication.
                </p>

                <h3>Security Recommendations</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Do not allow internal service accounts
                        to sign in through public user interfaces.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Separate internal administrative
                        authentication from public access.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Protect privileged credentials
                        using appropriate secret management.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Apply least-privilege permissions
                        to service accounts.
                    </li>
                </ul>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="password-strength-controls">

                <h2>Implement Proper Password Strength Controls</h2>

                <p>
                    Password strength controls help prevent
                    attackers from guessing passwords through
                    manual or automated attempts.
                </p>

                <h3>Password Length</h3>

                <p>
                    OWASP's guidance references a minimum
                    of 15 characters for password-only
                    authentication and 8 characters when
                    multi-factor authentication is enabled.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Applications should support passwords
                    of at least 64 characters to accommodate
                    longer passphrases.
                </p>

                <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>Encourage long passwords and passphrases.</li>
                    <li>Reject commonly used and compromised passwords.</li>
                    <li>Allow whitespace and Unicode characters.</li>
                    <li>Do not silently truncate passwords.</li>
                    <li>Avoid mandatory character-composition rules.</li>
                    <li>Provide password-strength feedback.</li>
                    <li>Do not require arbitrary periodic password changes.</li>
                    <li>Require password changes when compromise is identified.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Example: Password Length Validation</h3>

                <div class="code-example">
                    <pre><code>function isPasswordLengthValid(password) {
    return [...password].length >= 15;
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p>
                    This example demonstrates only a minimum
                    length check. A production password policy
                    also needs breached-password screening,
                    secure storage, and abuse protection.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="secure-password-recovery">

                <h2>Implement Secure Password Recovery Mechanism</h2>

                <p>
                    Password recovery allows users to
                    regain access to their accounts
                    when they forget their credentials.
                </p>

                <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>Use unpredictable recovery tokens.</li>
                    <li>Make recovery tokens expire.</li>
                    <li>Invalidate tokens after successful use.</li>
                    <li>Limit repeated recovery attempts.</li>
                    <li>Avoid revealing whether an account exists.</li>
                    <li>Notify users about important account changes.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Example: Recovery Token Generation</h3>

                <div class="code-example">
                    <pre><code>const crypto = require("node:crypto");

const resetToken =
    crypto.randomBytes(32).toString("hex");</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p>
                    A complete recovery system must also
                    store a secure token verifier, enforce
                    expiration and single use, and validate
                    requests on the server.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="secure-password-storage">

                <h2>Store Passwords in a Secure Fashion</h2>

                <p>
                    Applications must not store user
                    passwords as readable plaintext.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Passwords should be processed with
                    password-specific hashing algorithms
                    that incorporate salts and appropriate
                    computational costs.
                </p>

                <h3>Common Password Hashing Algorithms</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>Argon2id</li>
                    <li>scrypt</li>
                    <li>bcrypt</li>
                    <li>PBKDF2</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Example: bcrypt in Node.js</h3>

                <div class="code-example">
                    <pre><code>const bcrypt = require("bcryptjs");

async function hashPassword(password) {
    return await bcrypt.hash(password, 12);
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p>
                    This is an educational example.
                    The hashing algorithm and cost
                    parameters should be selected
                    according to current security guidance.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="safe-password-comparison">

                <h2>Compare Password Hashes Using Safe Functions</h2>

                <p>
                    Password verification should use
                    the comparison function provided
                    by the password hashing library.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Developers should avoid manually
                    comparing password hashes using
                    ordinary string comparison.
                </p>

                <h3>Example</h3>

                <div class="code-example">
                    <pre><code>const bcrypt = require("bcryptjs");

async function verifyPassword(
    enteredPassword,
    storedHash
) {
    return await bcrypt.compare(
        enteredPassword,
        storedHash
    );
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h3>Security Considerations</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>Use trusted password verification functions.</li>
                    <li>Protect against timing-related information leaks.</li>
                    <li>Apply reasonable input limits.</li>
                    <li>Avoid unsafe type conversions.</li>
                </ul>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="change-password-feature">

                <h2>Change Password Feature</h2>

                <p>
                    A password change is a sensitive
                    account operation that should
                    require appropriate identity verification.
                </p>

                <h3>Recommended Process</h3>

                <ol>
                    <li>Confirm the user has an authenticated session.</li>
                    <li>Request the current password or suitable re-authentication.</li>
                    <li>Validate the new password against the password policy.</li>
                    <li>Securely hash the new password.</li>
                    <li>Update the stored credential.</li>
                    <li>Consider revoking existing sessions.</li>
                    <li>Notify the user about the change.</li>
                </ol>

                <p>
                    Password change requests should
                    also be protected against cross-site
                    request forgery when cookie-based
                    sessions are used.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="chapter" id="password-transport-tls">

                <h2>Transmit Passwords Only Over TLS or Other Strong Transport</h2>

                <p>
                    Passwords and other authentication
                    credentials must be protected
                    while traveling between the
                    browser and server.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Transport Layer Security (TLS)
                    protects network communications
                    against interception and modification.
                </p>

                <h3>Example</h3>

                <div class="code-example">
                    <pre><code>https://example.com/login</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

                <ul>
                    <li>Use HTTPS for login and authenticated pages.</li>
                    <li>Configure TLS securely.</li>
                    <li>Protect authentication cookies.</li>
                    <li>Avoid placing passwords in URLs.</li>
                    <li>Use secure transport for password recovery.</li>
                </ul>

            </section>

<!-- ==========================================
     AUTHENTICATION - CONTINUATION
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="reauthentication-sensitive-features">

    <h2>Require Re-authentication for Sensitive Features</h2>

    <p>
        Some account operations require additional verification
        even when a user is already logged in.
    </p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Changing an account password</li>
        <li>Changing a registered email address</li>
        <li>Updating multi-factor authentication settings</li>
        <li>Accessing highly sensitive information</li>
        <li>Performing high-value transactions</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Re-authentication reduces the risk of misuse when
        an authenticated session has been compromised
        or left unattended.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="reauthentication-risk-events">

    <h2>Re-authentication After Risk Events</h2>

    <p>
        Re-authentication may be necessary when an application
        detects activity that suggests increased account risk.
    </p>

    <h3>When to Trigger Re-authentication</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Suspicious account activity</li>
        <li>Unexpected changes in login context</li>
        <li>Account recovery events</li>
        <li>Changes to authentication settings</li>
        <li>Attempts to perform sensitive operations</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Re-authentication Mechanisms</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Requesting the current password</li>
        <li>Requesting an additional authentication factor</li>
        <li>Using a passkey or hardware security key</li>
        <li>Applying a stronger authentication challenge</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Implementation Recommendations</h3>

    <p>
        Applications should consider the user's context,
        avoid unnecessary interruptions, and manage sessions
        securely after re-authentication.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="strong-transaction-authentication">

    <h2>Consider Strong Transaction Authentication</h2>

    <p>
        Sensitive transactions may require additional
        verification to confirm that the legitimate user
        intends to perform the operation.
    </p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Transferring funds</li>
        <li>Changing payment information</li>
        <li>Approving high-risk administrative actions</li>
        <li>Updating important account ownership details</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>TLS Client Authentication</h3>

    <p>
        Mutual TLS can authenticate both the server and client
        using digital certificates.
    </p>

    <p>
        It can be useful in managed enterprise environments
        where client certificates can be distributed
        and maintained securely.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="authentication-error-messages">

    <h2>Authentication and Error Messages</h2>

    <p>
        Authentication error messages should not reveal
        unnecessary information about user accounts.
    </p>

    <p>
        Detailed error messages may help attackers determine
        whether particular accounts exist.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Information-Revealing Message</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>Account not found.</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Better Generic Message</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>Invalid username or password.</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Authentication Responses</h3>

    <p>
        Login, account recovery, and registration workflows
        should avoid disclosing sensitive account-existence
        information.
    </p>

    <h3>Error Codes and URLs</h3>

    <p>
        Developers should also ensure that HTTP responses,
        redirect URLs, and response timing do not reveal
        information that generic messages are intended
        to conceal.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="automated-authentication-attacks">

    <h2>Protect Against Automated Attacks</h2>

    <p>
        Attackers may use automated tools to attempt
        large numbers of logins or test stolen credentials.
    </p>

    <h3>Common Attacks</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Brute Force:</strong>
            Repeatedly guessing passwords.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Credential Stuffing:</strong>
            Testing credentials exposed in other data breaches.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Password Spraying:</strong>
            Trying a small number of common passwords
            across many accounts.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Multi-Factor Authentication</h3>

    <p>
        MFA requires additional proof beyond a single password.
        Phishing-resistant authentication methods should
        be preferred where appropriate.
    </p>

    <h3>Login Throttling</h3>

    <p>
        Throttling limits repeated authentication attempts.
    </p>

    <h3>Account Lockout</h3>

    <p>
        Temporary restrictions may be used after repeated
        failed attempts, but account lockout policies
        should avoid creating opportunities for
        denial-of-service attacks.
    </p>

    <h3>CAPTCHA</h3>

    <p>
        CAPTCHA can increase the cost of automated attacks,
        but it should not be considered a complete
        security solution.
    </p>

    <h3>Security Questions and Memorable Words</h3>

    <p>
        Traditional knowledge-based security questions
        are not recommended as authentication or
        re-authentication factors.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="authentication-logging-monitoring">

    <h2>Logging and Monitoring</h2>

    <p>
        Authentication events should be monitored
        to detect attacks, unusual behavior, and repeated
        authentication failures.
    </p>

    <h3>Events to Monitor</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Failed login attempts</li>
        <li>Repeated password failures</li>
        <li>Account lockouts</li>
        <li>Suspicious authentication activity</li>
        <li>High-risk account changes</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Security logs should not contain plaintext passwords,
        authentication tokens, or other unnecessary secrets.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="passwordless-authentication-protocols">

    <h2>Use of Authentication Protocols That Require No Password</h2>

    <p>
        Modern applications may use federated identity systems,
        cryptographic authenticators, or delegated access
        protocols instead of requiring applications
        to collect user passwords directly.
    </p>

    <h3>OAuth 2.0 and 2.1</h3>

    <p>
        OAuth is an authorization framework that allows
        applications to obtain delegated access
        to protected resources.
    </p>

    <p>
        OAuth alone is not an authentication protocol.
    </p>

    <h3>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</h3>

    <p>
        OpenID Connect adds an identity layer to OAuth
        and supports authentication and single sign-on.
    </p>

    <h3>OIDC Implementation Guidance</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Validate ID token signatures.</li>
        <li>Validate token issuer and audience.</li>
        <li>Check token expiration.</li>
        <li>Use maintained authentication libraries.</li>
        <li>Follow the identity provider's security guidance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Secure Federated Account Linking</h3>

    <p>
        Federated accounts should be linked using verified
        identity information and appropriate
        account-owner confirmation.
    </p>

    <p>
        Applications should not automatically link accounts
        based only on matching email addresses.
    </p>

    <h3>SAML</h3>

    <p>
        Security Assertion Markup Language supports
        identity federation and single sign-on,
        particularly in enterprise environments.
    </p>

    <h3>FIDO</h3>

    <p>
        FIDO authentication uses public-key cryptography
        to support secure authentication.
    </p>

    <p>
        Modern FIDO2 technologies include WebAuthn and passkeys.
    </p>

    <h3>Hardware-backed Key Storage</h3>

    <p>
        Some authenticators protect private keys
        using secure hardware.
    </p>

    <p>
        However, developers should not assume every
        passkey or authenticator is hardware-backed.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="authentication-password-managers">

    <h2>Password Managers</h2>

    <p>
        Password managers help users generate, store,
        and fill different passwords for different websites.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Use standard HTML login fields.</li>
        <li>Allow password autofill.</li>
        <li>Allow users to paste passwords.</li>
        <li>Support long passwords.</li>
        <li>Provide logical keyboard navigation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>HTML Example</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>&lt;label for="login-email"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;input
    type="email"
    id="login-email"
    name="email"
    autocomplete="username"
&gt;

&lt;label for="login-password"&gt;Password&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;input
    type="password"
    id="login-password"
    name="password"
    autocomplete="current-password"
&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="changing-registered-email">

    <h2>Changing a User's Registered Email Address</h2>

    <p>
        Changing an account's registered email address
        is a sensitive operation because email is often
        used for account recovery.
    </p>

    <h3>If MFA Is Enabled</h3>

    <ol>
        <li>Validate the authenticated session.</li>
        <li>Explain the email change process.</li>
        <li>Collect and validate the proposed new address.</li>
        <li>Require MFA verification.</li>
        <li>Store the change as pending.</li>
        <li>Notify the current email address.</li>
        <li>Require confirmation from the new address.</li>
        <li>Complete the change only after required checks.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>If MFA Is Not Enabled</h3>

    <ol>
        <li>Validate the authenticated session.</li>
        <li>Request the new email address.</li>
        <li>Verify the user's current password.</li>
        <li>Keep the change pending.</li>
        <li>Require confirmation from the current address.</li>
        <li>Require confirmation from the proposed address.</li>
        <li>Complete the change after successful verification.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Additional Considerations</h3>

    <p>
        Confirmation tokens should be unpredictable,
        time-limited, and single-use.
    </p>

    <p>
        Administrators and support personnel should also
        be trained to recognize social engineering attempts.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="chapter" id="adaptive-risk-based-authentication">

    <h2>Adaptive or Risk Based Authentication</h2>

    <p>
        Adaptive authentication adjusts authentication
        requirements according to the risk associated
        with a particular request.
    </p>

    <h3>Possible Risk Signals</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Unrecognized devices</li>
        <li>Unexpected geographic locations</li>
        <li>Unusual login times</li>
        <li>Suspicious IP addresses</li>
        <li>Unusual behavioral patterns</li>
        <li>Sensitivity of the requested operation</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <p>
        A user signing in from a familiar device
        may complete the normal authentication process.
    </p>

    <p>
        If the same account is accessed from an unfamiliar
        device under suspicious circumstances, the application
        may require additional verification.
    </p>

    <h3>Possible Security Responses</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Allow the request.</li>
        <li>Require additional MFA verification.</li>
        <li>Apply an anti-automation challenge.</li>
        <li>Temporarily restrict the operation.</li>
        <li>Revoke a compromised session.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Risk signals should supplement authentication
        rather than replace proof of identity
        or a valid session.
    </p>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     END OF AUTHENTICATION
========================================== -->

<!-- ==========================================
     PART 2 - AUTHORIZATION
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="authorization-cheat-sheet">

    <h2>Authorization Cheat Sheet</h2>

    <p>
        Authorization determines whether an entity
        is permitted to perform a particular action
        or access a particular resource.
    </p>

    <p>
        Authentication verifies identity,
        while authorization determines permitted access.
    </p>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <p>
        A student may successfully log into an educational
        website, but that does not mean the student
        should have permission to manage other users' accounts.
    </p>

    <h3>Authentication vs. Authorization</h3>

    <table class="lesson-table">

        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Authentication</th>
                <th>Authorization</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>

        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Verifies identity.</td>
                <td>Verifies permissions.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Establishes who the user is.</td>
                <td>Determines what the user may do.</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Uses credentials or authenticators.</td>
                <td>Uses access-control policies.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>

    </table>

    <h3>Authorization Risks</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Unauthorized access to private records</li>
        <li>Unauthorized modification of data</li>
        <li>Privilege escalation</li>
        <li>Unauthorized administrative operations</li>
        <li>Exposure of restricted files</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="authorization-recommendations">

    <h2>Authorization Recommendations</h2>

    <p>
        Secure authorization requires clear access-control
        policies and consistent enforcement.
    </p>

    <p>
        Permissions should be designed, tested,
        and reviewed throughout the software
        development lifecycle.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="enforce-least-privileges">

    <h2>Enforce Least Privileges</h2>

    <p>
        The principle of least privilege means granting
        users only the permissions necessary
        to perform their intended tasks.
    </p>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <table class="lesson-table">

        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>User Role</th>
                <th>Allowed Actions</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>

        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Student</td>
                <td>
                    Read permitted books and manage
                    their own bookmarks.
                </td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Content Editor</td>
                <td>
                    Manage assigned educational content.
                </td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Administrator</td>
                <td>
                    Perform approved administrative operations.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>

    </table>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Define permissions during system design.</li>
        <li>Restrict access by role and resource ownership.</li>
        <li>Review privileges regularly.</li>
        <li>Remove permissions that are no longer needed.</li>
        <li>Test horizontal and vertical access restrictions.</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="authorization-deny-by-default">

    <h2>Deny by Default</h2>

    <p>
        Applications should deny access unless
        an explicit authorization rule permits
        the requested action.
    </p>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>function canAccessAdmin(user) {
    return user?.role === "admin";
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This simplified example denies access when
        the user is missing or does not have
        the required role.
    </p>

    <p>
        Real applications must enforce access control
        on the server and may require additional
        resource-specific conditions.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="validate-permissions-every-request">

    <h2>Validate the Permissions on Every Request</h2>

    <p>
        Authorization must be checked whenever
        a protected resource or operation is requested.
    </p>

    <p>
        Checking permissions only when the user
        first logs in is insufficient.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Express Middleware</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>function requireAdmin(req, res, next) {

    if (!req.session?.userId) {
        return res.sendStatus(401);
    }

    if (req.session.role !== "admin") {
        return res.sendStatus(403);
    }

    next();
}

app.get(
    "/api/admin",
    requireAdmin,
    (req, res) => {
        res.json({
            message: "Authorized"
        });
    }
);</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This example assumes the server has securely
        established the session role.
    </p>

    <p>
        Production systems should use trusted,
        current authorization data.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="review-authorization-technologies">

    <h2>
        Thoroughly Review the Authorization Logic
        of Chosen Tools and Technologies
    </h2>

    <p>
        Frameworks and libraries can provide
        authorization features, but their default
        configuration may not match an application's
        security requirements.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Understand framework authorization behavior.</li>
        <li>Review security configurations.</li>
        <li>Keep dependencies updated.</li>
        <li>Monitor dependency vulnerabilities.</li>
        <li>Apply defense in depth.</li>
        <li>Implement additional rules when necessary.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Developers remain responsible for ensuring
        that authorization policies are correctly enforced.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="attribute-relationship-access-control">

    <h2>
        Prefer Attribute and Relationship Based
        Access Control over RBAC
    </h2>

    <p>
        Access control can use roles, attributes,
        relationships, or combinations of these factors.
    </p>

    <h3>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</h3>

    <p>
        RBAC assigns permissions according to
        predefined roles, such as student,
        editor, or administrator.
    </p>

    <h3>Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)</h3>

    <p>
        ABAC considers attributes such as account status,
        resource classification, location, and other context.
    </p>

    <h3>Relationship-Based Access Control (ReBAC)</h3>

    <p>
        ReBAC considers relationships between users
        and resources, such as ownership or membership.
    </p>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>function canEditBookmark(user, bookmark) {

    return Boolean(
        user &&
        bookmark &&
        user.id === bookmark.ownerId
    );
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This illustrates an ownership relationship check.
        It must operate on trusted server-side information.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="protect-lookup-identifiers">

    <h2>
        Ensure Lookup IDs Are Not Accessible
        Even When Guessed or Tampered With
    </h2>

    <p>
        Attackers may attempt to access another user's
        records by changing identifiers in URLs
        or API requests.
    </p>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>/api/bookmarks/101

/api/bookmarks/102</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        Changing an identifier must not allow access
        to another user's private information.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Verify ownership of requested resources.</li>
        <li>Check permissions before returning data.</li>
        <li>Do not rely on unpredictable IDs alone.</li>
        <li>Validate user-supplied identifiers.</li>
        <li>Prevent unauthorized record modifications.</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="static-resource-authorization">

    <h2>Enforce Authorization Checks on Static Resources</h2>

    <p>
        Protected files must have appropriate
        access controls, even when they are images,
        documents, or other static resources.
    </p>

    <h3>Examples of Protected Resources</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Private user documents</li>
        <li>Restricted educational materials</li>
        <li>Personal account files</li>
        <li>Administrative exports</li>
        <li>Confidential reports</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Files requiring authorization should not
        be directly exposed through unrestricted
        public static-file directories.
    </p>

    <p>
        Instead, the server should validate
        permissions before serving protected content.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Protected Book Route</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>app.get("/read/:bookId", (req, res) => {

    if (!req.session?.userId) {
        return res.sendStatus(401);
    }

    const bookId = req.params.bookId;

    const unlockedBooks =
        req.session.unlockedBooks || {};

    if (!unlockedBooks[bookId]) {
        return res.sendStatus(403);
    }

    // Resolve the book from a trusted manifest.
    // Validate the resulting path and serve it
    // only from the protected-books directory.
});</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This simplified example illustrates session
        and book-unlock checks. A complete implementation
        must also validate the requested book ID,
        resolve its file safely, and prevent path traversal.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="authorization-check-location">

    <h2>
        Verify That Authorization Checks
        Are Performed in the Right Location
    </h2>

    <p>
        Authorization decisions should be enforced
        in trusted parts of the application.
    </p>

    <p>
        Client-side controls may improve the interface,
        but they cannot provide reliable
        security enforcement.
    </p>

    <h3>Insecure Approach</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>if (userRole === "admin") {
    adminButton.hidden = false;
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        Showing or hiding a button does not protect
        the server endpoint behind that button.
    </p>

    <h3>Secure Design Principle</h3>

    <p>
        Authorization must be checked by the server
        before executing protected operations
        or returning restricted information.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="authorization-failure-safe">

    <h2>Exit Safely When Authorization Checks Fail</h2>

    <p>
        When authorization fails, the application
        must stop the protected operation safely.
    </p>

    <p>
        A failed authorization check must not allow
        the request to continue into sensitive
        application logic.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Express Authorization Failure</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>function requireAuthenticatedUser(
    req,
    res,
    next
) {

    if (!req.session?.userId) {
        return res.status(401).json({
            success: false,
            message: "Authentication required."
        });
    }

    next();
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>HTTP Status Codes</h3>

    <table class="lesson-table">

        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Status</th>
                <th>Meaning</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>

        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>401</td>
                <td>
                    Authentication is required or invalid.
                </td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>403</td>
                <td>
                    The request is understood,
                    but access is forbidden.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>

    </table>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Stop processing unauthorized requests.</li>
        <li>Return an appropriate HTTP response.</li>
        <li>Do not expose protected information.</li>
        <li>Record security-relevant failures appropriately.</li>
        <li>Avoid leaking sensitive internal details.</li>
    </ul>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     END OF AUTHORIZATION
========================================== -->

<!-- ==========================================
     PART 3 - WEB SERVICE SECURITY
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="web-service-security-cheat-sheet">

    <h2>Web Service Security Cheat Sheet</h2>

    <p>
        Web services allow applications and systems
        to communicate over networks.
    </p>

    <p>
        Securing these services involves protecting
        data, authenticating communicating parties,
        enforcing authorization, and preventing
        resource-exhaustion attacks.
    </p>

    <h3>Common Security Objectives</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Confidentiality</li>
        <li>Integrity</li>
        <li>Authentication</li>
        <li>Authorization</li>
        <li>Availability</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="transport-confidentiality">

    <h2>Transport Confidentiality</h2>

    <p>
        Transport confidentiality protects information
        while it moves between web service clients
        and servers.
    </p>

    <p>
        TLS helps protect communications from interception
        and man-in-the-middle attacks.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Use HTTPS for sensitive communications.</li>
        <li>Use securely configured TLS.</li>
        <li>Protect authenticated sessions in transit.</li>
        <li>Encrypt communications between services.</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="web-service-server-authentication">

    <h2>Server Authentication</h2>

    <p>
        Server authentication helps clients verify
        that they are communicating with the intended service.
    </p>

    <h3>Certificate Validation</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Verify that the certificate is trusted.</li>
        <li>Check certificate expiration.</li>
        <li>Check applicable revocation status.</li>
        <li>Verify the certificate matches the service hostname.</li>
        <li>Validate the TLS handshake properly.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Applications should not disable certificate
        verification to bypass connection errors.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="web-service-user-authentication">

    <h2>User Authentication</h2>

    <p>
        Web services may authenticate users,
        applications, or other systems requesting access.
    </p>

    <h3>Authentication Methods</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Session-based authentication</li>
        <li>Token-based authentication</li>
        <li>Client certificates</li>
        <li>Mutual TLS</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        HTTP Basic Authentication transmits credentials
        using Base64 encoding, which is not encryption.
    </p>

    <p>
        OWASP does not recommend Basic Authentication.
        If it is used, it must be protected by TLS.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="transport-encoding">

    <h2>Transport Encoding</h2>

    <p>
        Web services must use compatible encoding
        conventions so that messages are interpreted correctly.
    </p>

    <p>
        In SOAP-based services, consistent encoding
        between client and server helps avoid unexpected
        parsing or processing behavior.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practice</h3>

    <p>
        Define and enforce the expected message encoding
        for communicating systems.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="message-integrity">

    <h2>Message Integrity</h2>

    <p>
        Message integrity helps determine whether information
        has been modified without authorization.
    </p>

    <p>
        TLS protects message integrity during transport.
    </p>

    <p>
        XML digital signatures can provide message-level
        integrity when signed XML documents need protection
        beyond the transport connection.
    </p>

    <h3>Important Concepts</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Digital signatures</li>
        <li>Public-key cryptography</li>
        <li>Signature verification</li>
        <li>Message tampering detection</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="message-confidentiality">

    <h2>Message Confidentiality</h2>

    <p>
        Message confidentiality prevents unauthorized parties
        from reading sensitive information.
    </p>

    <p>
        Sensitive messages should be encrypted using strong,
        appropriately configured cryptographic methods.
    </p>

    <h3>Transport vs. Message Encryption</h3>

    <table class="lesson-table">

        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Transport Encryption</th>
                <th>Message Encryption</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>

        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    Protects data while transmitted.
                </td>
                <td>
                    Protects selected message content.
                </td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>
                    Commonly implemented with TLS.
                </td>
                <td>
                    Can protect data after transport ends.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>

    </table>

    <p>
        When sensitive information must remain encrypted
        after receipt, transport encryption alone
        is insufficient.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="web-service-authorization">

    <h2>Authorization</h2>

    <p>
        Web services must verify that clients are permitted
        to perform requested operations and access
        requested resources.
    </p>

    <h3>Coarse-Grained Authorization</h3>

    <p>
        Determines whether a client may use
        a particular service or operation.
    </p>

    <h3>Fine-Grained Authorization</h3>

    <p>
        Determines whether a client may access
        specific records or perform specific actions
        on those records.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Check authorization on every request.</li>
        <li>Verify access to requested resources.</li>
        <li>Protect sensitive account operations.</li>
        <li>Restrict administrative functions.</li>
        <li>Separate management interfaces where appropriate.</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="schema-validation">

    <h2>Schema Validation</h2>

    <p>
        Schema validation checks whether incoming messages
        follow an expected structure.
    </p>

    <p>
        SOAP services commonly use XML Schema Definitions
        (XSD) to specify allowed message structures
        and data constraints.
    </p>

    <h3>Common Constraints</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Allowed data types</li>
        <li>Maximum field lengths</li>
        <li>Required fields</li>
        <li>Allowed character sets</li>
        <li>Accepted value patterns</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: XML Schema</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>&lt;xs:element name="username"&gt;
    &lt;xs:simpleType&gt;
        &lt;xs:restriction base="xs:string"&gt;
            &lt;xs:maxLength value="50"/&gt;
        &lt;/xs:restriction&gt;
    &lt;/xs:simpleType&gt;
&lt;/xs:element&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This fragment illustrates a maximum-length
        constraint in an XML schema.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="content-validation">

    <h2>Content Validation</h2>

    <p>
        Web services should validate received information
        before processing it.
    </p>

    <h3>Potential XML Threats</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Malformed XML content</li>
        <li>XML entity expansion attacks</li>
        <li>XML external entity attacks</li>
        <li>Unexpected input values</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Use allowlist-based validation where practical.</li>
        <li>Reject malformed input.</li>
        <li>Use securely configured XML parsers.</li>
        <li>Disable unnecessary external entity processing.</li>
        <li>Enforce resource and input limits.</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="web-service-output-encoding">

    <h2>Output Encoding</h2>

    <p>
        Output encoding ensures that untrusted information
        is interpreted as data rather than executable content.
    </p>

    <p>
        Correct encoding helps prevent cross-site scripting
        when web service responses are displayed in webpages.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Safe Text Display</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>const messageElement =
    document.getElementById("message");

messageElement.textContent =
    serverResponse.message;</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        Using textContent displays text without interpreting
        it as HTML markup.
    </p>

    <p>
        Encoding must match the output context,
        such as HTML text, attributes, JavaScript, or URLs.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="web-service-virus-protection">

    <h2>Virus Protection</h2>

    <p>
        Web services that accept file attachments
        may receive malicious files.
    </p>

    <p>
        File scanning helps detect known malware
        before attachments are stored or processed.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Scan uploaded attachments.</li>
        <li>Keep malware detection rules updated.</li>
        <li>Validate file types.</li>
        <li>Apply file-size restrictions.</li>
        <li>Protect file storage locations.</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="web-service-message-size">

    <h2>Message Size</h2>

    <p>
        Large requests can consume excessive server resources.
    </p>

    <p>
        Attackers may exploit unrestricted message sizes
        to degrade service performance or cause denial of service.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Express Request Limit</h3>

    <div class="code-example">
        <pre><code>app.use(
    express.json({
        limit: "100kb"
    })
);</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This example limits JSON request bodies.
        The appropriate limit depends on the application.
    </p>

    <p>
        SOAP/XML services need their own parser
        and message limits.
    </p>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="web-service-availability">

    <h2>Availability</h2>

    <p>
        Availability means ensuring that legitimate users
        can access a service when needed.
    </p>

    <p>
        Web services should protect against requests
        that consume excessive processing power,
        memory, or network capacity.
    </p>

    <h3>Resources Limiting</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Limit CPU consumption.</li>
        <li>Limit memory consumption.</li>
        <li>Limit simultaneous connections.</li>
        <li>Limit open files and processes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Message Throughput</h3>

    <p>
        Throughput describes how many requests a service
        processes during a particular time interval.
    </p>

    <p>
        Request-rate and execution-time limits should
        be based on tested capacity and operation cost.
    </p>

    <h3>XML Denial of Service Protection</h3>

    <p>
        XML processing may be abused through deeply nested
        structures, oversized messages, or excessive
        entity expansion.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Practices</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Limit XML document size.</li>
        <li>Restrict excessive nesting.</li>
        <li>Prevent unsafe entity expansion.</li>
        <li>Limit XML element-name lengths.</li>
        <li>Test parser behavior against malicious input.</li>
    </ul>

</section>


<section class="chapter" id="endpoint-security-profile">

    <h2>Endpoint Security Profile</h2>

    <p>
        A web service endpoint is a network-accessible
        location through which clients communicate
        with a service.
    </p>

    <p>
        Endpoint security requires independently enforced
        protections for authentication, authorization,
        transport security, and resource usage.
    </p>

    <h3>WS-I Basic Profile</h3>

    <p>
        The Web Services Interoperability Basic Profile
        focuses on compatibility between web service
        implementations.
    </p>

    <p>
        Conformance with an interoperability profile
        does not automatically mean a service is secure.
    </p>

    <h3>Important Security Controls</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Secure TLS configuration</li>
        <li>Server identity verification</li>
        <li>Appropriate client authentication</li>
        <li>Authorization on every protected request</li>
        <li>Input and schema validation</li>
        <li>Resource and request limits</li>
        <li>Security monitoring</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Final Reminder</h3>

    <p>
        Web service security depends on multiple
        independent protections working together.
    </p>

    <p>
        Authentication establishes identity,
        authorization controls permitted actions,
        and service security protects communications,
        processing, and availability.
    </p>

</section>

